C(1,[1,2,3],[4,5],PRO+':12–61','faisceau de Lie; coh. plats','For an elliptic scheme f:C→S, identity D and T=Lie(C/S), O(D)|D=T; R⁰,R¹f_*O(nD) commute with base change and have ranks (n,0) for n>0, (1,1) for n=0 and (0,−n) for n<0. The relative theorem needs the proper flat finite-presentation hypotheses; the manuscript works locally noetherian.','proof sketched','A',['EGAIII2','StacksBaseChange'],'EGA III2 Theorem7.7.5(II), Proposition7.7.10 and7.8.4 give base change; Proposition7.8.6 treats H⁰(O), and Corollary7.9.9 handles one-degree fibre cohomology. Exact date relation to undated sheets is not established.','EGA III2 published1963; present statement checked2026','P1: fibre RR, vanishing in complementary degrees, the unit section at n=0, and the explicitly cited relative theorem. Constant fibre ranks alone are not used over nonreduced bases.',['S62-002','S62-003','S62-030','S62-032'])
C(2,[4],[5],PRO+':50–61','très ample ... n≥3','O(nD) is relatively ample iff n≥1 and relatively very ample iff n≥3. The length-two vanishing proof is reconstructed, while the inequality glyph remains undecided.','proof sketched','E',['StacksVeryAmple'],'Classical curve Riemann–Roch; exact modern length-two proof in Lemma53.22.5. No novel general-base proof is written here.','Classical; modern verification2026','P1 gives the length-two calculation deg=n−2>0 and excludes degrees1,2.',['S62-001'])
C(3,[5,10],[6,8],PRO+':67–89;141–155','E₀=E₁=O; universellement injective','E_n=f_*O(nD) has E₀=E₁=O, gr₁=0 and gr_n=T^n for n≥2; nonnegative filtration inclusions are universally injective and graded multiplication is tensor multiplication.','proved there','B',['StacksBaseChange','StacksWeierstrass'],'Established pole-filtration construction; base-change and Weierstrass sources match its hypotheses. No separate first-publication claim.','Established by the modern sources; priority not fixed','P1, divisor exact sequences and locally free cokernels.',['S62-001','S62-003'])
C(4,[6],[7,9,10],PRO+':95–101;161–206','E₃ engendre Γ_*','The filtered algebra O_C(*D) is locally generated by pole orders2,3; the homogeneous section ring needs degrees1,2,3 and a degree6 relation. Literal generation by degree3 alone is false for the homogeneous ring.','proof sketched','B',['StacksWeierstrass'],'The modern general Weierstrass construction gives the same generators; the filtered/homogeneous ambiguity is editorial.','Checked2026; historical priority not assigned','P1 leading-term induction; seven monomials versus rank6.',['S62-001'])
C(5,[7,8,9],[7,8],PRO+':103–139','reste à définir; ssi p premier à n','Invariant differentiation extends to O(*D), raises the pole order by≤1, and acts on the nth graded piece by −n. Thus invertibility requires n a unit; p|n kills the graded map in characteristic p.','open on the pages','B',['StacksWeierstrass'],'A local derivation calculation settles the flagged definition. It does not assert restricted-Lie or Dieudonné consequences.','Checked2026; elementary calculation, no priority claim','P1 and math_checks pole derivative rows.',['S62-001','S62-002'])
C(6,[11,12,13,14],[9,10,11],PRO+':161–225','tangente d’inflexion; préciser ce pt','|3D| embeds C as a relative plane cubic; D is its flex point, tangent cuts3D, and its ideal is generated by a primitive cubic locally. The homogenization requires eyz² and P(E₃) has dimension2, not3.','proof sketched','B',['StacksWeierstrass','StacksVeryAmple'],'Exact atlas and cubic embedding are established in Lemma105.6.1 and the length-two criterion. The missing term/exponent are reading alarms.','Checked2026','P1; degree and homogenization checks, no analytic computation.',['S62-001'])
C(7,[15,16,17,18,20,21],[11,12,13,14],PRO+':211–279','représentable par M; fibré principal','Elliptic curves with an admissible flex frame form the open six-coefficient scheme; Lie-compatible normalization gives the usual five-coefficient Weierstrass atlas. Its triangular coordinate-change group yields a smooth torsor and a quotient-stack presentation; μ²=λ³ implies λ=u²,μ=u³.','proof sketched','B',['StacksWeierstrass','StacksQuotient'],'Stacks105.6.1–2 explicitly supplies the Weierstrass atlas and quotient interpretation. A fixed splitting would have a smaller stabilizer than the written triangular group.','Established modern formulation; checked2026','P1; unit identity u=μ/λ, preserved pole weights.',['S62-001'])
C(8,[15],[11],PRO+':211–218','Ici le discriminant s’explicite : —','For the primitive flex cubic, smoothness over S is equivalent to invertibility of its discriminant, not merely being a nonzero element. In standard Weierstrass coordinates Δ=−b₂²b₈−8b₄³−27b₆²+9b₂b₄b₆, with the conventional b_i.','stated without proof','B',['StacksWeierstrass'],'The discriminant polynomial and open condition are explicitly displayed by Stacks§105.6. The manuscript leaves its formula blank.','Checked2026','P1; math_checks exactly verifies the full general Weierstrass discriminant by completing the square and comparing the cubic discriminant with16Δ. The manuscript leaves the expansion blank.',['S62-001'])
C(9,[19],[13],PRO+':252','raisonnement général ... genre g','A general-genus analogue requires a fixed bounded embedding and specified genus/marking conditions. Hilbert schemes and a projective coordinate group supply parameter presentations, but the sentence does not determine one universal precise theorem.','stated without proof','C',['GrothHilbert1961'],'Exposé221 Theorem3.2 constructs the projective fixed-Hilbert-polynomial parameter scheme; the unspecified moduli problem remains to be formulated.','Bourbaki talk/publication1961; no priority comparison with undated leaf','No numerical computation; exact construction scope compared to Hilbert Theorem3.2.',['S62-013','S62-014','S62-015'],'medium')
C(10,[22],[15],PRO+':284','Il ne semble ... moins de paramètres','No precise minimum-parameter theorem is identifiable without fixing allowed atlases, coordinate groups, rigidification and topology. The page expresses a tentative expectation, not a moduli-dimension assertion.','open on the pages','F',['StacksWeierstrass','AOV2008'],'Weierstrass atlas and rigidification searches show several inequivalent meanings of parameter count; none settles the undefined optimality claim.','Searched through2026-11-19','No computation can decide an unspecified optimization class.',['S62-002','S62-006','S62-013'],'high')
C(11,[23],[15],PRO+':284','δ=1; 12.ième ... 24.ième','An invertible weight12 discriminant is normalized after a finite free faithfully flat cover adjoining its twelfth root. The cover is étale if12 is invertible. The 24th-root parenthesis is not identified.','stated without proof','B',['StacksKummer','StacksWeierstrass'],'Kummer sequence and Weierstrass weights give the exact finite-flat construction.','Checked2026; original parenthesis undecided','P1: monic root algebra and derivative12X¹¹.',['S62-001','S62-003'])
C(12,[24,26,27,28,30,31],[16,17],PRO+':289–330','x²=y³+ay²+by+c; groupe affine','For2 invertible, inversion splits E₃ into E₂ and an odd line and gives a square-equals-cubic equation; for3 invertible the quadratic cubic coefficient is removed. Inversion fixes E₂ in all characteristics. The diagonal weights2,3 group with one translation has central μ₂, so is not Aff₁ itself.','proof sketched','B',['StacksWeierstrass'],'General Weierstrass transformations establish the normalizations; the Aff₁ identification needs quotient by μ₂.','Checked2026','P1 and P2; complete-square/depress-cubic formulas; math_checks depression.',['S62-001','S62-006'])
C(13,[25,67],[16,52,54],PRO+':304–306; '+K1+':11–13;53–58','X−s∞ est ... homogène','The affine complement of the marked infinity, rather than the whole proper quotient C/{±1}, is a torsor under a line bundle. Scan52 explicitly supplies that correction.','corrected by himself later','B',['StacksWeierstrass','Hua2026'],'The relative quotient construction is standard; Hua§50 distinguishes the general-base trisection statement from his field calculation.','Correction in supplied continuation; Hua preprintNov2026','P2: proper P¹ cannot be affine-line torsor; continuation read at both endpoints.',['S62-001','S62-017'])
C(14,[65,67],[52,54],K1+':11–13;53–58','somme nulle; barycentre','A torsor under a commutative group with multiplication by n invertible has a canonical barycentre for a finite étale n-section. Invert3 to center an étale trisection and obtain a line bundle with a trace-zero cubic divisor.','proof sketched','B',['Hua2026','StacksQuotient'],'Hua§50 records these equivalent descriptions but explicitly leaves their scheme descent outside Lean. Our descent proof is P2.','Manuscript continuation undated; modern preprintNov2026','P2 origin-change calculation; no numerical computation.',['S62-017'])
C(15,[32,33],[19,48],PRO+':338–440;1195–1197','Si A ss élémts nilpotts; H¹(S,Z/3)','Kummer classifications use μ_n torsors; replacing μ₃ by constant C₃ requires a chosen primitive cube root. Reducedness is needed when passing from a special invariant equation to the reduced exceptional orbit.','false as stated','B',['StacksKummer','StacksQuotient'],'Kummer and quotient-stack formulations retain the missing hypotheses. The field-only Hua result does not remove them.','Checked2026','P2 counterexamples over Q and k[ε]/ε²; math_checks special fibre expansions.',['S62-003','S62-017'])
C(16,[34,35],[19],PRO+':410–440','27(j−1)/j (x+1)','The tentative cubic normal form uses incompatible discriminant and invariant conventions; a reliable family is y²=4x³−h(x+1), h=27j_classical/(j_classical−1728), only where the denominators and discriminant are units.','false as stated','B',['DLMF233','DLMF2315','StacksWeierstrass'],'Standard cubic invariant formulas determine the corrected constants; the scratch formula is not a new family over its singular parameter values.','Checked2026','math_checks cubic discriminant, general i and h family; P2.',['S62-001','S62-004'])
C(17,[36,37],[21,23,25],PRO+':485–526;571–602;638–684','Δ=b³−27c²; i','For x³+bx+c, Disc=−4b³−27c² and Res(f,f′)=4b³+27c². For x³+ax²+bx+c, p=b−a²/3, q=c−ab/3+2a³/27, i=−27q²/Disc=1−j_classical/1728. The coefficient9ab is required in27q.','false as stated','E',['DLMF233','DLMF2315'],'Classical Weierstrass invariants, exactly compatible with DLMF§23.3. The manuscript’s displayed b³ coefficient and sign are incorrect literally.','Classical before the sheets; attested references1927','math_checks exact resultant, discriminant, depression, identity.',['S62-004'])
C(18,[38,39],[25,28],PRO+':647–684;739–775','t=(x₃−x₁)/(x₃−x₂); S₃','The six anharmonic transformations give S₃ on P¹−{0,1,∞}; the manuscript table uses left-to-right application. The involution σ=(t−2)/(2t−1) is defined when3 is invertible and centralizes this S₃.','proved there','E',['Beauville2010','Hua2026'],'Classical finite PGL₂ action; Hua Thm50.1 matches the split field invariant. Matrix computations are independently repeated.','Beauville preprint2009/publication2010 as modern reference; classical action predates manuscript','math_checks all six maps, complete multiplication table, determinant3 and σ².',['S62-006','S62-007','S62-017'])
C(19,[40,41,42],[30,32],PRO+':810–837;860–866','Prouver : normalisateur ... S₃×Z/2','Over every base with6 invertible, the normalizer group scheme in PGL₂ of the standard constantS₃ is S₃×C₂, with extra generatorσ. The cyclic subgroup fixes the roots of t²−t+1; its torus is twisted by the discriminant−3.','open on the pages','B',['Beauville2010'],'Beauville§4 gives the field dihedral centralizer C₂. P2 now independently proves the arbitrary-base fppf-sheaf statement by commutator/anticommutator equations, including nonreduced test rings; this strengthening is not attributed to the field reference.','arXiv2009-09-22; published2010','P2 relative normalizer reproof and math_checks exact matrix equations.',['S62-006','S62-007','S62-008'])
C(20,[43,44,45,46,49],[32,34,36,38],PRO+':860–889;919–999','ramification; G_mS; normalisée','The degree3 cyclic quotient branches only at the two C₃-fixed points; the harmonic triples branch in the subsequent degree2 quotient. The proper quotient is P¹, and only the complement of its zero/infinity is a G_m torsor. A map is unique only after target and value normalization.','false as stated','B',['Beauville2010','Hua2026'],'The tame action and explicit invariant give the branch indices. Standard torsor formalism applies to the open complement.','Checked2026','math_checks derivative of f=F/(3√−3 t(t−1)); P2.',['S62-007','S62-017'])
C(21,[36,46,47,48],[38,40],PRO+':953–999;1037–1062','i=−1/27 ...; σ″','For i=−F²/(27t²(t−1)²), 1−i=4(t²−t+1)³/(27t²(t−1)²); ramification indices are2,3,2 over0,1,∞. Over √−3, f²=i and σ sends f↦1/f and i↦1/i.','proved there','E',['Hua2026','DLMF2315'],'Hua Thm50.1 is an exact field-level match; its certificate was not executed here. Our polynomial identities are independent.','Classical invariant; Hua circulationNov2026','math_checks exact identities, derivatives and substitutions; P2.',['S62-004','S62-017'])
C(22,[50,51,54,55,64,70],[42,44,50],PRO+':1084–1088;1117–1127;1232–1256','trisection; σ.t=σ̄(t)','Pairs consisting of a genus-zero curve and an étale trisection are classified by S₃ torsors. Adding a disjoint section gives [U/S₃], with U=P¹−{0,1,∞}. The generic free locus is the affine invariant open i(i−1) invertible.','proof sketched','B',['StacksQuotient','Hua2026'],'Stacks§95.15 supplies the exact torsor-plus-equivariant-map formalism; Hua explicitly does not certify this general-base descent.','Modern quotient formulation checked2026','P2 descent on objects and morphisms; math_checks free/exceptional stabilizer equations.',['S62-001','S62-017'])
C(23,[56,57],[46],PRO+':1154–1160','normalisation; partout définie','On normal noetherian integral S, generic separable cubic data have finite normalization but require everywhere étaleness; use a product algebra for split data. If the full ordering torsor extends étale, a regular invariant forces its equivariant U-map to extend uniquely by integrality.','open on the pages','B',['StacksNormalFinite','SGA1'],'Finite-normalization and purity references specify the separate hypotheses; purity requires regularity, which the manuscript does not assume.','SGA1 seminar1960–61/publication1971; modern normalization lemma checked2026','P3 integrality extension proof.',['S62-001','S62-009','S62-010','S62-016'])
C(24,[58,59],[19,46],PRO+':338–345;1158–1160','quels i conviennent ??','Over a DVR with6 invertible, local tame lifting near0 requires even v(i), and near1 requires3|v(i−1), with unit-root descent data. On a singular normal base divisorial tests alone fail: k[[x,y,z]]/(xy−z²), i=x.','open on the pages','B',['SGA1','StacksKummer'],'SGA1 X.3.1 proves purity for regular targets, not merely normal ones. This precisely bounds divisorial sufficiency.','SGA1 seminar1960–61,book1971; counterexample checked2026','P3 checks normality, parity, strict henselianity and ramification at the closed point.',['S62-003','S62-009','S62-016'])
C(25,[32,33,60],[48],PRO+':1195–1197','H¹(S,Z/2); H¹(S,Z/3)','At i=0 and i=1 the reduced exceptional loci are residual gerbes. BC₂ works over a reduced base; BC₃ additionally requires splitting the cyclotomic stabilizer. Nonreduced fibre data t=−1+ε or ρ+ε refute the unrestricted classifications.','false as stated','B',['StacksQuotient','StacksKummer','Hua2026'],'Quotient-stack theory keeps the infinitesimal thickening; the field-level special-fibre theorem does not imply a scheme-level gerbe equality.','Checked2026','P2; math_checks vanishing orders2 and3, arithmetic μ₃ example.',['S62-001','S62-003','S62-017'])
C(26,[63,72],[48],PRO+':1201–1207','it²; √i','For i invertible the intermediate degree2 quotient is u↦iu². Its fixed bisection has square class i. The sign quotient of the S₃ ordering torsor has square class−3i and need not coincide with it.','proved there','B',['StacksKummer','Hua2026'],'Kummer exactness matches the quadratic classes; the distinction is independently reconstructed from the alternating polynomial.','Checked2026','math_checks r²=−27i and sign under both transpositions; P2.',['S62-003','S62-017'])
C(27,[65,71],[16,42,50,52,54],PRO+':304–306;1084;1232–1256; '+K1+':11–13;53–58','conique, trisection et section','The trisection construction forgets central elliptic μ₂. Over6 invertible the lift is a μ₂ gerbe: if L is the centred cubic line, an elliptic lift requires M²≅L. Also O(D)⊗ω of fibre degree1 splits the Brauer obstruction of a conic with trisection.','stated without proof','B',['AOV2008','StacksKummer','StacksWeierstrass'],'AOV AppendixA supplies rigidification; the specific square-root line description is our reconstruction checked by coordinate weights.','AOV preprint2007-03-11/publication2008; application checked2026','P2 weight and degree calculations. This is an earlier-run implication, not a manuscript gerbe theorem.',['S62-001','S62-003','S62-013','S62-015','S62-016'])
C(28,[66],[50],PRO+':1253–1256','π₁(S)=0; Donn. non canonique','For S the spectrum of a complete local ring with algebraically closed residue field, every finite étale torsor splits. Choosing a split frame gives anharmonic orbits of t with t(t−1) invertible, with stabilizers retained.','stated without proof','B',['StacksHenselian','StacksEtaleHenselian'],'Complete-local henselianity and the finite-étale residue equivalence prove the precise assertion.','Checked2026','P2 descent plus opened Stacks Theorem59.32.3 and Lemma10.153.7.',['S62-003'])
C(29,[74],[20],PRO+':453–479','Lemme d’Artin-Rees','For noetherian A, ideal I, finite module E and submodule F, IⁿE∩F=I^(n−c)(IᶜE∩F) for n≥c. Induced and intrinsic I-adic topologies coincide.','proof sketched','E',['StacksArtinRees'],'The named Artin–Rees lemma is precisely Stacks10.51.2; the retained Rees proof stops before its last finite-generation step.','Standard named theorem before the reused typescript; exact first circulation not established','P4 independent Rees-module completion of the proof.',['S62-003','S62-006'])
C(30,[75,76,96],[22],PRO+':552–567','toute série ... tend vers0','For a countably filtered module with ultrametric topology, completeness is equivalent to convergence of every series whose terms tend to0. Closed submodules and arbitrary quotient modules are complete, allowing nonseparated quotients.','proved there','E',['StacksTopological'],'The exact linear-topology completion setting is Stacks87.4.3–5; completeness and separatedness must not be conflated.','Classical topological algebra; current comparison2026','P4 Cauchy subsequence and lifted-difference proof.',['S62-006','S62-010','S62-011'])
C(31,[76,77,78],[24,26],PRO+':613–634;706–716','p,q≤n; conv. simple des coefficients','The (I,X)-adic topology on A[[X]] equals coefficientwise I-adic product topology; coefficient j<n lies in I^(n−j). Completeness passes from A to A[[X]], with no separatedness assumption in this convention. The printed p,q≤n inclusion is false; ≥n repairs it.','false as stated','E',['StacksTopological','StacksCompletion'],'Standard completion/power-series results use precisely these finite coefficient conditions.','Classical; checked2026','P4 explicit filtration counterexample and coefficientwise proof.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-010'])
C(32,[79],[26],PRO+':720–735','E complet; F séparé; G(u) surjectif','For exhaustive bounded-below decreasing filtrations, a filtered map E→F from complete E to separated F with surjective associated graded is strict surjective. Only its hypotheses survive on this sheet; the conclusion is reconstructed.','stated without proof','B',['StacksTopological','StacksCompletion'],'The successive-lifting argument is established filtered algebra; the source fragment does not identify a stronger claim.','Modern statement checked2026; reused typescript priority unassigned','P4 independent error-correction proof.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-010'])
C(33,[80],[41],PRO+':1070–1080','E/E_n→Ê/Ê_n bijectifs','Separated completion preserves every filtration quotient and the associated graded; continuous multiplication and module actions extend. These quotients are discrete, not necessarily finite.','proved there','E',['StacksTopological','StacksCompletion'],'Stacks87.4 and10.97.4 give exact completion quotient statements.','Classical; checked2026','P4 inverse-limit quotient argument.',['S62-009','S62-010'])
C(34,[81,82,83],[43,45],PRO+':1102–1111;1142–1148','homomorphismes topologiques; composé','Separated completion preserves a strict exact sequence of metrizable abelian groups. Mere continuity does not suffice, and arbitrary composites of strict maps need not be strict. The extension target in scan45 must be completed unless already complete.','false as stated','E',['StacksTopological'],'The exact linear-topology result is Stacks87.4.3–5. The broader metrizable-group form is independently reproved with strictness.','Classical; checked2026','P4 Q_discrete→Q_usual counterexample and Z→R→R/Z composition counterexample, all topologies specified.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-010'])
C(35,[84],[45,47],PRO+':1134;1191','U(g) ... noethérien','If K is commutative noetherian and g is finite as a K-module, U(g) is left/right noetherian. Without finite generation, an infinite-dimensional abelian g gives the nonnoetherian polynomial ring in infinitely many variables.','false as stated','B',['StacksCompletion'],'The general filtered-noetherian method matches the proof; P4 checks the enveloping-algebra specialization without needing PBW injectivity. A missing standing hypothesis remains an ink/context question.','Checked2026; precise original authorial scope unknown','P4 filtered quotient Sym(g) and ascending-variable-ideal counterexample.',['S62-006','S62-009'])
C(36,[85,86],[47,49],PRO+':1171–1189;1215–1223','G(E) noethérien; A[[X]]','If A is complete and E separated for exhaustive bounded-below filtrations, finite/noetherian gr E implies finite/noetherian E. Thus noetherian A has noetherian finite-variable formal power series, and an I-adically separated complete A with I finite and A/I noetherian is noetherian.','proved there','E',['StacksCompletion'],'Exact complete-adic corollary is Stacks10.97.5; the proof uses the same successive graded lifts.','Classical; checked2026','P4 independent lifting and submodule argument; missing Σ in source is a reading alarm.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-011'])
