C(38,[95],[51,53,55],K1+':7;22–47;66–92','Ê=Â⊗E; Â plat','For noetherian A and finite E, I-adic completion is Â⊗E, is exact on finite modules, and Â is flat over A. If I⊂Jac(A), it is faithfully flat; the tensor identity itself needs no Jacobson hypothesis.','proved there','E',['StacksCompletion'],'Precisely Stacks10.97.1–3. The continuation completes the earlier fragment.','Classical; checked2026','P4 Artin–Rees/inverse-limit/finite-presentation argument checked step by step.',['S62-009','S62-010'])
C(39,[95],[53,55],K1+':22–47;66–92','F=ÂF∩E; sommes intersections quotients','Under the Zariski/noetherian hypotheses, completion preserves finite sums/intersections and colon constructions with finite generators; finite submodules recover by intersection with E under faithful flatness. Â is complete noetherian and (Â,IÂ) is a Zariski pair.','proof sketched','E',['StacksCompletion'],'Stacks10.97.3–5 and flat base change justify the exact finite statements. A colon by an infinitely generated object is not asserted.','Classical; checked2026','P4 flat exactness applied to kernels and finite presentations.',['S62-009','S62-010'])
C(40,[],[57],K1+':103–183','Dξu ... 4u³+bu+c','Over a base with6 invertible, for a Lie-frame ξ and compatible centred pole-two u, the cubic has (Dξu)²=4u³+bu+c; replacing ξ by λξ gives u↦λ²u,b↦λ⁴b,c↦λ⁶c. Unclear scratch discriminant powers are not resolved by these weights.','proof sketched','B',['StacksWeierstrass','DLMF233'],'The weights agree with the standard Weierstrass transformation and analytic invariants after distinguishing period scaling from frame scaling.','Checked2026','P1 leading poles and weight count; math_checks cubic equation conventions.',['S62-001','S62-004'])
C(41,[],[58],K1+':197–254','on trouve λ_i=0','The base-change/Nakayama scratch can prove generation after reduction under finite hypotheses. Linear independence over A needs a flat or faithful/injective hypothesis not supplied by merely showing coefficients vanish in B. No complete theorem is identifiable from the fragment.','open on the pages','F',['StacksCompletion'],'Searches of base-change/completion statements do not repair missing hypotheses uniquely. Faithful-flat descent is a possible reconstruction, not the text.','Searched2026-11-19','P4: A→A/J kills nonzero coefficients; this is a precise obstruction to the written inference.',['S62-003','S62-009','S62-010'],'medium')
C(42,[88,89],[29,31],PRO+':783–805;844–858','deux à deux indépendantes','Finitely many incomparable valuation rings admit the residue-separation needed to count prolongations. Arbitrary simultaneous approximation requires independence; distinct higher-rank valuations can share a nontrivial coarsening.','proved there','E',['ADF2020'],'ADF Theorem2.8 matches independent approximation; Proposition6.2 records compatible dependent variants and traces them to Nagata1953/Ribenboim1957.','Classical antecedents in ADF bibliography; preprint2019/publication2020','P5 common-u-adic-coarsening counterexample separates the hypotheses.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-010','S62-012'])
C(43,[89,97],[31,33,35],PRO+':844–858;873–881;896–914','prolongement; maximal','Valuations/places extend to algebraic field extensions via maximal local subrings. Prolongation valuation rings are localizations of the integral closure and that closure is their intersection; in a finite extension their number is at most the degree.','proof sketched','E',['StacksValuations','StacksEF','ADF2020'],'Stacks10.50.2–5 and15.125.2 give the maximal-local and degree bounds; the integral-closure statement is checked by P5.','Classical Krull valuation theory; modern verification2026','P5 Zorn/place proof and residue/value independence.',['S62-006','S62-009','S62-010'])
C(44,[92,93,94],[35,37,39],PRO+':896–914;935–949;1007–1033','ef≤[L:K]; quotient torsion','For algebraic valued L/K, Γ_L/Γ_K is torsion and the residue extension algebraic; convex subgroups correspond, preserving rank. For finite L/K, ef≤[L:K] and discreteness is preserved in both directions; infinite algebraic extensions can lose discreteness.','proved there','E',['StacksEF','StacksValuations'],'Exact fundamental inequality is Stacks15.125.2. The torsion/convex-group conclusions follow from the same polynomial-value argument.','Classical; checked2026','P5 independent least-valuation/residue proof; union of k((t^(1/n!))) counterexample to an infinite-extension overreach.',['S62-003','S62-006','S62-009','S62-010'])

# Classical claim families. Separate errors are indexed in readings.tsv; no ink is changed.
def E(n,scans,loc,his,statement,status,refs,fate,comp,search):
    C(n,[],scans,loc,his,statement,status,'E',refs,fate,'Known before the manuscript; DLMF points to Whittaker–Watson1927 where applicable; earliest circulation not asserted',comp,search)
E(45,[60,75],K1+':263–271; '+K2+':311–334','deux périodes distinctes; linéairement indépendantes','A nonconstant elliptic function is meromorphic and periodic for a rank-two lattice; real independence is essential. The period group is discrete of rank2. The earlier distinct-period wording is insufficient, as exp(z) shows; scan75 supplies the stronger definition.','corrected by himself later',['Sutherland2023','DLMF232'],'Sutherland Definition14.1 and DLMF23.2(i) fix the exact lattice hypothesis.','P6 exp(z) counterexample and discreteness argument; no numerical test.',['S62-004','S62-019','S62-020'])
E(46,[60,61],K1+':267–293','somme des résidus nulle; au moins deux poles','On C/Λ, a nonconstant meromorphic function has equal positive zero/pole degree≥2, zero total residue, and divisor sum0 moduloΛ. Equal divisors imply constant ratio; equal principal parts imply constant difference. Boundary counts require the usual representatives or edge/corner fractions.','proof sketched',['Sutherland2023','Dummit2023'],'The exact general properties are proved in Sutherland§14.1 and Dummit slides4–20.','P6 contour and compactness proof.',['S62-019','S62-020'])
E(47,[61,75,76,78,79],K1+':291–293; '+K2+':311–324;339–355;376–421','dépendent algébriquement; R₁(p)+p′R₂(p)','For a fixed lattice, any two elliptic functions are algebraically dependent and C(Λ) is finite over C(f) for nonconstant f. Every even elliptic function is rational in wp; uniquely every elliptic function is R₁(wp)+wp′R₂(wp). If all poles lie inΛ, R₁,R₂ are polynomials.','proved there',['Dummit2023','Sutherland2023'],'Dummit’s Weierstrass-function-field theorem matches the exact decomposition; finite extension is the nonconstant finite map of compact curves.','P6 even quotient and odd division, including halfperiod regularity.',['S62-019','S62-020'])
E(48,[62,64,75,76,79],K1+':299–310;378–394; '+K2+':327–355;398–421','p(z)=1/z²+...','The regularized lattice sum defines the unique even elliptic wp with principal part z^−2 and zero constant term; ζ has the regularized simple-pole sum and σ the canonical product. The coefficient of z^(2n) is (2n+1)Σ_(ω≠0)ω^(−2n−2). Halfperiod-versus-period factors must be tracked.','proof sketched',['DLMF232','DLMF239','Sutherland2023'],'DLMF23.2.4–6 and23.9.1–3; Sutherland Theorem14.28.','P6 normal convergence and paired sums; math_checks recurrence; reading index catches inconsistent factors.',['S62-004','S62-012','S62-020'])
E(49,[62,79,80,81,91],K1+':305–335; '+K2+':418–454; '+K3+':436–505','p′²=4p³−g₂p−g₃','wp′²=4wp³−g₂wp−g₃=4∏(wp−e_i), g₂=20c₁=60Σω^−4, g₃=28c₂=140Σω^−6; e₁+e₂+e₃=0, g₂=−4Σe_ie_j, g₃=4e₁e₂e₃. Missing squares, wrong factor powers and g₁/20c₂ on later leaves are false literally.','false as stated',['DLMF233','DLMF239'],'DLMF23.3.1–5,23.3.10 and23.9 supply each invariant identity with the same normalizations.','P6 Laurent proof; math_checks exact coefficient recurrence and discriminants.',['S62-004','S62-012','S62-015'])
E(50,[62,80,91],K1+':314–335; '+K2+':425–440; '+K3+':480–498','inverse ... intégrale; racines','wp is locally the inverse of the cubic elliptic integral with consistent sign and path; its halfperiod values are the three distinct cubic roots. wp″=6wp²−g₂/2 and wp‴=12wp·wp′; higher even derivatives are polynomials in wp, odd derivatives wp′ times polynomials.','proof sketched',['DLMF233','DLMF236'],'DLMF23.3.12–13 and23.6(iv) match the ODE and inverse-integral constructions.','P6 ODE differentiation and branch qualification; math_checks halfperiod translation.',['S62-004','S62-015'])
E(51,[62,63,64,81,82],K1+':337–409; '+K2+':455–510','ζ impaire; σ paire','ζ′=−wp, σ′/σ=ζ; ζ andσ are odd. ζ has lattice poles of residue1;σ has simple lattice zeros, σ′(0)=1. Literal sigma paire and the positive primitive wording are incompatible with these normalizations.','false as stated',['DLMF232','DLMF239'],'DLMF23.2.5–8 fixes signs and parity;23.9 gives the power-series normalization.','P6 lattice/product proof; math_checks parity and sigma series.',['S62-004','S62-012','S62-013'])
E(52,[63,64,81,83],K1+':345–362;404–409; '+K2+':468–478; '+K3+':5','2η; relation de Legendre; e^(...+2iπ)','With Im(ω₂/ω₁)>0, ζ(z+2ω_i)=ζ(z)+2η_i, η₁ω₂−η₂ω₁=πi/2, and σ(z+2ω_i)=−e^(2η_i(z+ω_i))σ(z). ω₃=−ω₁−ω₂ gives η₁+η₂+η₃=0. The lone ω₂ shift and +2iπ exponent are incorrect literally.','false as stated',['DLMF232'],'DLMF23.2.11–17 gives quasiperiodicity and Legendre’s relation; translate its ω₃ into the manuscript’s ω₂.','P6 contour proof; math_checks both sigma quasiperiods.',['S62-004','S62-013'])
E(53,[63,82],K1+':364–374; '+K2+':489–501','décomposition de Hermite','At a pole a, a term c_r(z−a)^−r for r≥2 is reproduced by c_r(−1)^(r−2)wp^(r−2)(z−a)/(r−1)!, while residue terms use ζ(z−a). Their sum plus a constant is the elliptic function; residues sum0. The printed missing factorial and derivative order k_i are alarms.','false as stated',['DLMF232','Dummit2023'],'The standard principal-part construction uses DLMF’s normalized wp/ζ; the exact coefficient is independently derived, so no unverified historical theorem number is supplied.','P6 coefficient of wp^(r−2) and subtraction of all principal parts; symbolic differentiation is in math_checks pole rows.',['S62-004','S62-018','S62-019','S62-020'])
E(54,[65,83],K1+':413–433; '+K3+':9–29','formule de Jacobi','A degree-zero divisor with equal sums moduloΛ is realized by a sigma quotient. If Ω=sum poles−sum zeros=2n₁ω₁+2n₂ω₂, its unshifted quotient needs exp(−2(n₁η₁+n₂η₂)z). The positive exponent on scan65 conflicts with its two preceding shifted formulas.','false as stated',['DLMF232','Dummit2023'],'Classical divisor criterion and sigma quasiperiodicity give the exact sign; scan83 repeats the equal-sum version.','P6 multiplier computation, not a silent normalization; math_checks quasiperiod factors.',['S62-004','S62-019','S62-020'])
E(55,[65,83,84],K1+':435–443; '+K3+':33–127','formules d’addition','wp(u)−wp(v)=−σ(u+v)σ(u−v)/(σ²u σ²v); ζ(u+v)=ζu+ζv+(wp′u−wp′v)/(2(wpu−wpv)); wp(u+v)=−wpu−wpv+[(wp′u−wp′v)/(2(wpu−wpv))]². Duplication follows by the limit;4wp(2u)=Σ_(w=0,ω₁,ω₂,ω₁+ω₂)wp(u+w).','proof sketched',['DLMF2310'],'DLMF23.10.1–4 supplies addition and duplication identities.','P6 principal-part proof; math_checks all addition, duplication and four-translate identities, numerical residuals labelled.',['S62-004'])
E(56,[66],K1+':449–474','Z=(12p−(5−k²))/(12p−(5k²−1))','For k²≠0,1 the displayed Möbius function of wp with e₁=(1+k²)/6,e₂=−(k²+6k+1)/12,e₃=−(k²−6k+1)/12 solves Z′²=(1−Z²)(1−k²Z²). The chosen z₀ with wp(z₀)=(5−k²)/12,wp′(z₀)=−(1−k²)/2 gives Z(0)=0,Z′(0)=1.','proved there',['DLMF2213','DLMF236'],'This is an elementary alternative birational parametrization of the standard Jacobi ODE; DLMF fixes that ODE and the Weierstrass cubic.','math_checks exact rational ODE certificate and initial derivative, no reliance on a numerical match.',['S62-004','S62-005'])
E(57,[67,68,71],K1+':480–514; '+K2+':5–34;136–183','sn²=1/(p−e₁)','For the scan67 root ordering e₁=−(1+k²)/3,e₂=(2−k²)/3,e₃=(2k²−1)/3, sn²=1/(wp−e₁), with a globally meromorphic root fixed by sn′(0)=1. The halfperiod product is (wp−e₁)(wp(z−ω₁)−e₁)=k²; sigma formulas fix the square-root sign and shifted reciprocal.','proof sketched',['DLMF236','DLMF2213'],'DLMF23.6(iii) gives the same Weierstrass/Jacobi relation after permuting root labels.','P6 square-root/sigma argument; math_checks exact halfperiod product and ODE.',['S62-004','S62-005'])
E(58,[68,69,72,73],K2+':5–97;191–274','périodes; résidus; sn(z+2ω₂)=snz','For the early root ordering, sn has periods2ω₁,4ω₂ and changes sign under2ω₂; cn anddn have the period/antiperiod rows obtained by their sigma ratios. The isolated sn sign and dn period-summary lines conflict with their own correct tables; zero/pole cosets must include both listed representatives.','false as stated',['DLMF224'],'DLMF22.4 Tables22.4.1–3 give exact lattices and divisors; compare after translating the manuscript’s halfperiod ordering.','P6 theta-quotient parity argument; math_checks period tests in standard K,iK′ conventions.',['S62-005'])
E(59,[69,70,72,73,74],K2+':40–131;191–303','cn²; dn²; sn′=cn dn','With standard normalization, cn²=1−sn²,dn²=1−k²sn² and derivatives are sn′=cn dn,cn′=−sn dn,dn′=−k²sn cn. Generalized roots replace1,k² by e₂−e₁,e₃−e₁. Differentiating yields all listed second-order equations. Scan73 must contain sn(z), not bare z, in its quartic ODE.','false as stated',['DLMF226','DLMF2213'],'DLMF22.6.1 and22.13 give the full differential/algebraic system.','P6 differentiation of squared relations; math_checks ODE, derivatives and squares.',['S62-005','S62-012'])
E(60,[70,72],K2+':131;216–232','ρ=k/√(1−k²); résidus','Recasting cn as a scaled translated Jacobi sine gives squared modulus−k²/(1−k²); the literal real square-root expression misses i. Generalized cn₁/dn₁ residues simplify to reciprocal square roots of root differences, not their square roots; their first displayed fractions permit direct checking.','false as stated',['DLMF2213','DLMF236'],'The exact cubic differential systems determine the modulus and residues, independently of ambiguous handwritten simplifications.','P6 local pole expansion; the modulus square follows by factoring the cn ODE.',['S62-004','S62-005'])
E(61,[77],K2+':359–372','[deleted] recouvre ... nombre pair','The cancelled topological argument that every nonconstant torus→P¹ map has even degree is false: wp′ has one pole of order3, hence degree3. The live even-function theorem is separately proved on scans76,78.','false as stated',['Sutherland2023','Dummit2023'],'Sutherland Corollary14.27 explicitly gives wp′ order3; this checks a cancelled attempt, not a surviving assertion.','P6 degree counterexample with every compactness/meromorphic hypothesis satisfied.',['S62-019','S62-020'])
E(62,[81,82],K2+':444–510','c₃=c₁²/3; σ=u−...','For wp=z^−2+c₁z²+c₂z⁴+…, c₃=c₁²/3,c₄=3c₁c₂/11; ζ=1/z−c₁z³/3−c₂z⁵/5−… and σ=z−c₁z⁵/12−c₂z⁷/30+…. The first c₂ in scan81’s ζ expansion should mathematically be c₁.','false as stated',['DLMF239'],'DLMF23.9.2–7 gives the recurrences after shifting its coefficient index by1.','math_checks exact Laurent recurrence and exponential-series calculation.',['S62-012'])
E(63,[84,85],K3+':99–169','σ_i; √(p−e_i)','The halfperiod sigma functions σ_i are even, normalized to1 at0, and σ_i/σ is a meromorphic square root of wp−e_i. Its period2ω_i and antiperiods in the other halfperiod directions are compatible; the sentence claiming both signs at the same2ω_i is false literally.','false as stated',['DLMF232','DLMF236'],'DLMF23.6.10–15 and sigma quasiperiodicity match these normalized functions.','P6 halfperiod translation and local normalization; math_checks sigma laws.',['S62-004','S62-015'])
E(64,[85,86,89],K3+':151–229;342–394','Θ(v|t); A_(n+1)=−h^nA_n','Θ(v|τ)=2Σ_(n≥1)(−1)^(n−1)q^((n−1/2)²)sin((2n−1)πv), q=e^(πiτ). It is entire in v, holomorphic for Imτ>0, odd, has simple lattice zeros, and is determined up to scalar by its quasi-laws. The bilateral coefficient recurrence is −q^(2n), not−q^n.','false as stated',['DLMF202'],'DLMF20.2.1 and20.2.6 supply Fourier series and quasiperiodicity, with argumentπv.','P7 reindexing and normal convergence; math_checks period multipliers.',['S62-005'])
E(65,[86,88],K3+':195–229;292–335','σ par Θ; t+4,t+8','σ(u)=2ω₁ e^(η₁u²/(2ω₁))Θ(u/(2ω₁)|τ)/Θ′(0|τ); 2η₁ω₁=−Θ‴(0)/(6Θ′(0)). Θ(v|τ+4)=−Θ(v|τ), Θ(v|τ+8)=Θ(v|τ). The exponential must retain its squared argument.','false as stated',['DLMF236','DLMF207'],'DLMF23.6.8–9 and20.7.26 give the exact sigma/theta and lattice-parameter formulas.','P7 normalization; math_checks sigma construction and modular Fourier factors.',['S62-004','S62-005','S62-014','S62-015'])
E(66,[87],K3+':241–268','Σe^(−an²+bn+c)=−...Θ','For Re a>0, Σ_(n∈Z)e^(−an²+bn+c)=−e^(c+b/2−a/4)Θ((b−a−iπ)/(2iπ)|ia/π). The literal manuscript identity is correct after a bilateral index shift.','proof sketched',['DLMF202'],'This exact formula is an elementary reindexing of DLMF20.2.1; a superficially suspicious phase is not a reading error.','P7 bilateral derivation and math_checks Gaussian identity residual<10^−60 at the recorded parameters.',['S62-005'])
E(67,[87,88],K3+':270–335','Θvv=4iπΘt; H′/H=−1/(2t)','Θ_vv=4πiΘ_τ and Θ(v|τ)=i√(i/τ)e^(−πiv²/τ)Θ(v/τ|−1/τ), with the analytic upper-half-plane square-root branch. The derivative-ratio formula is valid; evaluating an odd function at0 cannot determine its scalar. The boxed final law is correct despite the conflicting intermediate a.','false as stated',['DLMF2013','DLMF207'],'DLMF20.13.1 and20.7.30 give precisely these transformations after argumentπv and oddness.','P7 differentiated Fourier series; math_checks heat and S law, branch explicitly specified.',['S62-005','S62-014'])
E(68,[89],K3+':342–350','Re T>1 ou Re(1/T)>1','For ReT>0 the asserted dichotomy is false: T=1/2+i gives real parts1/2 and2/5. Full modular reduction guarantees Imτ≥√3/2 and |q|≤exp(−π√3/2)<0.1, not universal Imτ≥1.','false as stated',['DLMF2322'],'DLMF23.22(ii) gives the actual modular reduction bound.','P7 counterexamples and math_checks exact rational real parts.',['S62-004'])
E(69,[89,90],K3+':353–423','Θ₁,Θ₂,Θ₃; e₃ ...1/2','The manuscript even functions Θ₁,Θ₂,Θ₃ are θ₂,θ₄,θ₃ at argumentπv. Their Fourier signs/exponents must match these series; Θ′(0)=πΘ₁(0)Θ₂(0)Θ₃(0). Halfperiod roots are quartic-theta combinations with denominator3, not2. Their logarithmic formulas use Θ_i″(0)/Θ_i(0), not the undefined Θ_i‴/Θ_i′.','false as stated',['DLMF202','DLMF236'],'DLMF20.2.2–4 and23.6.2–7 give the exact identities and root ordering.','P7 parity/constant-term test; math_checks all three root formulas and theta derivative.',['S62-004','S62-005','S62-015'])
E(70,[91,92],K3+':436–522','g₂,g₃ homogènes ...2,3','Under scaling periods by a, g₂ scales by a^−4 and g₃ by a^−6. The local coefficient-frame weights2,3 or4,6 elsewhere use a different action. Unregularized halfperiod sums and doubled full-lattice sums are not interchangeable.','false as stated',['DLMF233','DLMF2310'],'DLMF23.3.1–2 and23.10(iv) supply the homogeneity; the literal positive period weights are false.','P7 scaling every lattice summand, and P6 regularization.',['S62-004'])
E(71,[92,93],K3+':514–541','J=g₂³/Δ; J(i)=0','J=g₂³/(g₂³−27g₃²)=j_classical/1728 is SL₂(Z)-invariant and classifies lattices up to complex homothety. J(i)=1 and J(exp(πi/3))=0, with local ramification indices2 and3. The source exchanges these values. Its Picard allusion does not specify another theorem.','false as stated',['DLMF2315','Sutherland2023L15'],'DLMF§23.15 fixes the convention; Sutherland Theorem15.5, Lemma15.9 and Corollary15.12 prove lattice classification and uniformization. The earlier anharmonic invariant equals1−J.','P7 square/equianharmonic symmetries; math_checks i=1−j/1728 normalization.',['S62-004','S62-013','S62-019','S62-020','S62-022','S62-024','S62-025'])
E(72,[94],K3+':550–614','développements; dégénérescences','The Jacobi system with sn(0)=0,cn(0)=dn(0)=1 yields sn=z−(1+k²)z³/6+(1+14k²+k⁴)z⁵/120−(1+135k²+135k⁴+k⁶)z⁷/5040+…. At k=0 use sin,cos,1; at k=1 use tanh,sech,sech. The source dn=1/tanh is false at0.','false as stated',['DLMF2213','DLMF225'],'DLMF22.13 and22.5(ii) match the differential system and degenerations.','math_checks exact recurrence throughz⁹, initial-value degeneration counterexample; P6.',['S62-005'])
E(73,[94,95],K3+':605–692','périodes; zéros; poles; K″','For0<k<1, sn has lattice4K Z+2iK′Z; cn has4K Z+(2K+2iK′)Z; dn has2K Z+4iK′Z. Each has order2 on its own lattice and order4 on the common4K,4iK′ lattice. Zeros, poles and residues follow the tabulated translates; the no-real-zero margin and missing K′ primes are false literally.','false as stated',['DLMF224','DLMF225'],'DLMF22.4 Tables22.4.1–3 and22.5.1 fix the exact period and divisor data.','P6 theta divisors; math_checks all primitive/anti-period tests; source table checked entrywise.',['S62-005'])
E(74,[95],K3+':659–692','u+K; u+iK′; transformations imaginaires','Jacobi shifts by K,iK′ and K+iK′ and imaginary transforms follow with k′=√(1−k²), fixed positive for real0<k<1. For example sn(u+K)=cn u/dn u, sn(u+iK′)=1/(k sn u), sn(iu,k)=i sn(u,k′)/cn(u,k′); with K″=−K−iK′ the corrected dn shift is dn(u+K″)=−ik′sn(u)/cn(u). The source plus sign is false.','false as stated',['DLMF224','DLMF226'],'DLMF22.4(iii),22.6(iv) give the precise quarter-period and imaginary transformations.','P6 algebraic composition of shifts; math_checks all shifts, finite value tables, three imaginary formulas and a numerical counterexample to the literal dn(K″) sign.',['S62-005','S62-012'])
E(75,[96],K3+':701–733','sn=Mσ/σ₂; dn ...σ₁','For rectangular halfperiods ω₁>0,ω₂∈iR_+, use e₂<e₃<e₁, M²=e₁−e₂, k²=(e₃−e₂)/(e₁−e₂), K=Mω₁,iK′=Mω₂ and wp(u)=e₂+M²/sn²(Mu). Sigma ratios give sn=Mσ/σ₂, cn=σ₁/σ₂ and dn=σ₃/σ₂; the written dn denominatorσ₁ is wrong.','false as stated',['DLMF236'],'DLMF23.6(iii) is an exact match after swapping DLMF root/halfperiod indices2,3.','P6 squared equations and value at0; math_checks halfperiod product; pole divisor distinguishes σ₁ versusσ₂.',['S62-004','S62-015'])
E(76,[97],K3+':739–770','fonctions de Legendre et Θ; formules d’addition','With v=u/(2K), q=e^(−πK′/K), sn=(θ₃(0)/θ₂(0))θ₁(πv)/θ₄(πv), cn=(θ₄(0)/θ₂(0))θ₂(πv)/θ₄(πv), dn=(θ₄(0)/θ₃(0))θ₃(πv)/θ₄(πv). All three addition formulas have common denominator1−k²sn²u sn²v and the standard signed numerators.','stated without proof',['DLMF222','DLMF228'],'DLMF22.2.4–6 and22.8.1–3 give precisely the ratios and addition laws.','P6 theta/ODE justification; math_checks all three addition identities, separately labelled numerical.',['S62-005','S62-021'])

# Additional exact scope checks discovered while comparing the full continuation.
C(77,[],[60,61,62],K1+':263–343','Toute ... même nombre; fonction paire','A nonconstant elliptic function takes every value with the same multiplicity, equal to its pole degree; wp is the degree2 map C/Λ→P¹ and the inverse integral uniformizes the nonsingular cubic. Constant functions must be excluded from degree and discreteness claims.','proof sketched','E',['Sutherland2023','Sutherland2023L15','Dummit2023'],'Sutherland§14.1 gives degree facts; Theorem15.1 and Corollary15.12 give torus/cubic uniformization. Dummit supplies the field-theorem argument.','Classical; exact modern proof checked2026','P6 argument principle and cubic ODE.',['S62-019','S62-020','S62-022','S62-024','S62-025'])

C(78,[52,53],[42],PRO+':1084–1088','on restitue X′ [à préciser]; par descente une bisection','Over6 invertible, the centralizing involutionσ descends to every genus-zero curve with étale trisectionD, takesD to a disjoint associated trisectionD′, and applying the construction twice returnsD with the sameσ. Its fixed divisor descends to an étale bisection.','open on the pages','B',['StacksQuotient','Beauville2010','Hua2026'],'Torsor descent supplies the exact relative construction. P2 checks the author’s same-involution point on objects and coordinates. Hua certifies only the field calculation.','Checked2026; no separate first-publication claim','P2 conjugation/descent argument; math_checks σ² and fixed quadratic.',['S62-001','S62-007','S62-017'])
C(79,[90,91],[33,35],PRO+':873–875;903–914','une place ... à valeurs dans k qui prolonge g','For a unital homomorphism g:S→k from a subring of a fieldK into an algebraically closed fieldk, there exists a placeK→k∪{∞} extendingg. Equivalently, every field extensionL/k admits a k-valued place restricting toid_k.','proved there','E',['Deuring1959','Gardam2024'],'The exact general quantifiers occur in Deuring Lecture3 §5 pp12–13 and Gardam Theorem1.97 pp21–22. The typed proof via a maximal subextension and algebraicity matches them.','Attested in Deuring1959; modern lecture course2023–24; manuscript date undetermined','P5 Zorn/maximal-place proof checked, with residue target fixed; no numerical computation.',['S62-033','S62-034','S62-035','S62-036'])

# Refined statement IDs make refutation events unambiguous without inventing extra manuscript claims.
for claim in claims:
    if claim['status_on_pages']=='false as stated':
        claim['refined_statement']={'id':claim['id']+'.R','statement':claim['statement'],'authorship':'explicit reconstruction in this run; no ink emendation'}
for claim in claims:
    if 'AOV2008' in claim['refs']:
        claim['search_ids'] += ['S62-023','S62-027','S62-028','S62-029']
        claim['reference_scope_note']='Published AppendixA TheoremA.1 p1087 opened; the2014 corrigendum concerns Lemma2.14 and Proposition3.6, not this rigidification statement.'
# Newly opened exact primary discussion for the enveloping-algebra finiteness issue.
